selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#pyskyfire-viz\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.viz</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-viz\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">viz</span></code> turns simulation data into plots, reports, and geometry views. It is where the package moves from numerical results to things a user can inspect visually.</p>", "a[href=\"#example-versus-validation-entry\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">4. Example versus validation entry<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#example-versus-validation-entry\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The differences between examples and validation cases are important.</p><p>An <strong>example</strong> primarily teaches usage. A good example says:</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-pump-pyskyfire-turbine\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.pump</span></code> / <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.turbine</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-pump-pyskyfire-turbine\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>The <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pump</span></code> and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">turbine</span></code> subpackages contain tools for turbopumps. They are both under development, and currently the pyskyfire package works fine without them. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pump</span></code> sizes radial impellers and generates their blade geometry. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">turbine</span></code> designs axial turbines at mean radius (<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AxialTurbine</span></code>), describes them as hardware (<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">TurbineDefinition</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">BladeRow</span></code>) and generates their blades. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pump.PumpDuty</span></code> and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">turbine.TurbineDuty</span></code> state what each machine is asked to do. Volute manifolds live in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts</span></code>, and the meridional passage both machine types share lives in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.common</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#init-py\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">__init__.py</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#init-py\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>The top-level file <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">src/pyskyfire/__init__.py</span></code> marks <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire</span></code> as a Python package and exposes the main subpackages.</p>", "a[href=\"#how-the-subpackages-fit-together\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">3. How the subpackages fit together<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#how-the-subpackages-fit-together\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The subpackages each have a responsibility, but in order to run a regenerative cooling analysis or a full cycle analysis, the packages work together. A typical regenerative-cooling analysis might work like this:</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-ducts\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-ducts\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts</span></code> holds duct hydraulics that no single component owns: the friction\nphysics shared by cooling channels and piping, explicit pipe geometry,\nannular ducts, and volute and collector manifolds. It depends only on <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">common</span></code> for planar section\nprimitives and on <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">thermo</span></code> for fluid state records, and it imports from neither\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">regen</span></code> nor <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">network</span></code>, so cooling channels, turbopump hardware and engine-cycle\nadapters can all build on it without a circular import.</p>", "a[href=\"../howto/heat-exchangers.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Build a path-based heat exchanger<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#build-a-path-based-heat-exchanger\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.heat_exchanger</span></code> supports both prescribed surrounding gas and two\nfluids coupled through a wall. Channels use the same <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts.Path</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">PathNode</span></code>,\nand <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">PathStation</span></code> objects as pipes, and the existing rounded/squared sections.\nAll geometry angles are radians. Lengths are metres.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-heat-exchanger\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.heat_exchanger</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-heat-exchanger\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">circuit.py</span></code> defines channel banks, prescribed gas, and explicit thermal/flow\nconnections. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">solver.py</span></code> couples segment heat rates to enthalpy and static\npressure. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">chamber.py</span></code> places assemblies in prescribed chamber flow and reports\ndisplaced volume. Paths come from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts.path</span></code>, sections from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">regen.cross_section</span></code>,\nand scalar correlations from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">common</span></code>. See <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../howto/heat-exchangers.html\"><span class=\"std std-doc\">Heat exchangers</span></a>.</p>", "a[href=\"#package-structure\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Package Structure<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#package-structure\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>This page explains how the Pyskyfire repository is organised. It is written for users who are comfortable with engineering and Python scripts, but who may not have spent much time inside a Python package before.</p><p>The goal of this page is to give you a mental map of where things live, what each part is responsible for, and how the different parts of the project fit together.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-network\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.network</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-network\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">network</span></code> connects component models from other subpackages into a full engine-cycle calculation. Unlike <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">common</span></code>, it sits above the other subpackages: it is free to import from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">regen</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">thermo</span></code>, and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">common</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-injector\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.injector</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-injector\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">injector</span></code> holds injector hardware and the flow through it. It depends on\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts</span></code>, whose <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Orifice</span></code> is every hole, and on <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">thermo</span></code>; the engine network\nadapts it through <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">network.InjectorBlock</span></code>. Each element type is its own class.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-regen\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.regen</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-regen\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">regen</span></code> contains the thrust-chamber and regenerative-cooling model. This is one of the central subpackages in Pyskyfire.</p><p>A useful mental model is:</p>", "a[href=\"#how-to-read-the-code-without-getting-lost\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">5. How to read the code without getting lost<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#how-to-read-the-code-without-getting-lost\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>Pyskyfire is mostly written in an <strong>object-oriented</strong> style. This means that the code is organised around objects that represent things in the problem.</p><p>An object combines data and behaviour. For example, a thrust chamber is not only a list of numbers. It has geometry, wall layers, cooling circuits, coolant properties, and hot-gas properties. It also has methods that answer questions such as \u201cwhat is the local channel area?\u201d or \u201cwhat is the wall thickness here?\u201d</p>", "a[href=\"#the-repository-as-a-whole\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">1. The repository as a whole<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#the-repository-as-a-whole\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>When you open the Pyskyfire repository, you are looking at more than the Python code itself. A software repository usually contains:</p>", "a[href=\"#thermo-migration-unreleased\"]": "<h4 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Thermo migration (unreleased)<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#thermo-migration-unreleased\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h4><p>Update imports directly; no backwards aliases are provided. Paths below are\nrelative to <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire</span></code>:</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-thermo\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-thermo\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">thermo</span></code> owns fluid definitions, thermodynamic state resolution, transport properties, and combustion-gas aerothermodynamics. It connects engine geometry and propellant choices to the fluid properties needed by the rest of the package; coolant-specific heat-transfer policy remains in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">regen</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-common\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.common</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-common\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">common</span></code> holds dependency-free primitives that any other subpackage can use. It does not import from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">regen</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pump</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">turbine</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">network</span></code>, or <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">viz</span></code> \u2014 this keeps it a true leaf package and avoids circular imports. Fluid definitions and property models belong to <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">thermo</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#summary\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">6. Summary<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#summary\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>Pyskyfire is organised like a typical Python engineering package:</p>", "a[href=\"#src-pyskyfire\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">2. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">src/pyskyfire/</span></code><a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#src-pyskyfire\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">src/pyskyfire/</span></code> directory contains the code that becomes the <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire</span></code> Python package after installation.</p><p>The package content is as follows:</p>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
